Reference passing across function calls
=======================================

Passing a `&T` to a function temporarily shares ownership:
the callee's parameter holds the reference for the duration
of the call. The compiler must increment the refcount on
the way in (so the callee's binding is balanced by the
release at function exit), and the caller's binding stays
alive across the call.

Subtle cases:

  - **Passing the only ref to a function:** if the caller
    has the only reference and passes it, the refcount goes
    from 1 to 2 on entry and back to 1 on exit. If the
    increment is missing, the caller's view appears to be
    freed mid-call when the callee's "drop" runs.

  - **Returning a freshly-allocated ref:** the callee's
    `new T(...)` produces refcount=1 (owned). On return,
    no extra increment is needed — ownership transfers to
    the caller.

  - **Returning a borrowed ref:** the callee got the ref
    from an argument or some other source it doesn't own.
    The return path must increment so the caller's binding
    has its own share.

  - **Nested calls:** A call chain `f -> g -> h` passing
    the same ref must maintain a consistent count. If `g`
    drops too aggressively, `h` sees freed memory; if `g`
    fails to drop, the ref leaks.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct NrpBox
        v: int

Simple pass — caller still sees the ref after the call
-------------------------------------------------------

    inspect_box(b: &NrpBox) -> int = b.v

    #test
    test_pass_then_read() -> unit
        val b: &NrpBox = new NrpBox(42)
        val n = inspect_box(b)
        assert(n == 42, s"inspect_box should return 42, got $n")
        // Caller's b must still be alive
        assert(b.v == 42, s"b.v should still be 42 after the call, got ${b.v}")

Pass to a mutating callee
-------------------------

    increment_box(b: &NrpBox) -> unit
        b.v = b.v + 1

    #test
    test_pass_to_mutator() -> unit
        val b: &NrpBox = new NrpBox(10)
        increment_box(b)
        increment_box(b)
        increment_box(b)
        assert(b.v == 13, s"b.v should be 13 after 3 increments, got ${b.v}")

Nested call chain
-----------------

If any link in the chain mis-handles the refcount, by the time
we reach `nrp_read_box` the buffer is either freed or has the wrong
shared count.

    nrp_read_box(b: &NrpBox) -> int = b.v

    pass_through(b: &NrpBox) -> int = nrp_read_box(b)

    pass_through_2(b: &NrpBox) -> int = pass_through(b)

    #test
    test_nested_call_chain() -> unit
        val b: &NrpBox = new NrpBox(77)
        val n = pass_through_2(b)
        assert(n == 77, s"chained pass-through should return 77, got $n")
        assert(b.v == 77, s"b.v should still be 77 after nested calls, got ${b.v}")

Callee returns a fresh ref — ownership transfers
------------------------------------------------

`new NrpBox(...)` is owned; the return must NOT add an extra incr.
A double-incr would prevent the buffer from ever being freed (leak);
a missing decr at caller's scope-exit would do the same.

    make_box(v: int) -> &NrpBox = new NrpBox(v)

    #test
    test_returns_fresh_ref() -> unit
        val b: &NrpBox = make_box(99)
        assert(b.v == 99, s"b.v from make_box should be 99, got ${b.v}")
        b.v = 100
        assert(b.v == 100, s"b.v should be 100 after mutation, got ${b.v}")

Callee returns a borrowed ref — caller takes a new share
--------------------------------------------------------

The returned ref isn't `new`, so the return path must increment for
the caller's binding. Without that increment, the callee's drop
(the function-local share decr) frees the buffer before the caller
can read it.

    pass_through_ref(b: &NrpBox) -> &NrpBox = b

    #test
    test_returns_borrowed_ref() -> unit
        val orig: &NrpBox = new NrpBox(55)
        val borrowed: &NrpBox = pass_through_ref(orig)
        assert(borrowed.v == 55, s"borrowed.v should be 55, got ${borrowed.v}")
        // orig and borrowed share the buffer — mutate through one, see through the other
        borrowed.v = 66
        assert(orig.v == 66, s"orig.v should be 66 after borrowed.v=66, got ${orig.v}")

Many calls in a row don't leak
------------------------------

If the per-call balance is off by even one (extra inc, missing dec),
1000 calls produce 1000 extra references on the buffer. Final read
should still work, but the test exercises the allocator path under
repeated traffic.

    #test
    test_many_pass_throughs() -> unit
        val b: &NrpBox = new NrpBox(0)
        var i = 0
        while i < 1000
            increment_box(b)
            i = i + 1
        assert(b.v == 1000, s"b.v should be 1000 after 1000 increments, got ${b.v}")
